.SH NAME
what \- identify SCCS files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwhat\fI files\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B What\^
searches the given files for all occurrences of the pattern that
.BR get (1)
substitutes for %Z% (\fB@(#)\fR )
and prints out what follows until the first
.BR ~ ,
.BR > ,
new-line,
.BR \e ,
or null character.
.PP
.B What\^
is intended to be used in conjunction with the SCCS command
.BR get (1),
which automatically inserts identifying information,
but it can also be used where the information is inserted manually.
.SH EXAMPLES
For example, if the C program in file \fBf.c\fR contains
.PP
.RS 5
char ident[] = "\|@(#)identification information\|";
.RE
.PP
and \fBf.c\fR is compiled to yield \fBf.o\fR and \fBa.out\fR,
then the command
.PP
.RS 5
what\|
.RB f . c\|
.RB f . o\|
.RB a . out
.RE
.PP
will print
.PP
.RS 5
.TP 8
f\fB.\fRc:
.br
identification information
.TP 8
f\fB.\fRo:
.br
identification information
.TP 8
a\fB.\fRout:
.br
identification information
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
get(1),
help(1).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Use
.BR help (1)
for explanations.
.SH LIMITATIONS
It's possible that an unintended occurrence of the pattern
.B @(#)
could be found just by chance, but this causes no harm in
most cases.
